README-NEWS -- three news systems, and which one to takeA system runs one news system.  Take MNews with nn to read news incomfort: threads, kill files, online help, no cio, and all built from thesource on this disk.  Take C News with rn for the Unix pair.  TakeUUCPbb's news if you already run UUCPbb for mail.THE THREE---------                   UUCPbb 2.1 news     C News + rn         MNews + nn  ------------------------------------------------------------------------  directory        CMDS/UUCP           CMDS/NEWS           CMDS/MNEWS  programs         6                   12                  11, and `setup'  bytes            116146              342080              410966  needs cio        yes, all six        the six tools only  no  files articles   rnews               relaynews           inews (rnews                                                           hands on to it)  reader           readnews: prompts   rn: full screen     nn: full screen  posts            postnews            Pnews               inews, nnaux  spool            SPOOL/news          SPOOL/CNEWS         SPOOL/MNEWS  article names    COMP/OS/OS9/a1      junk/1              comp/os/os9/1  active file in   SYS/UUCP            USR/LIB/CNEWS       USR/LIB/NEWS  your place       .newsrc             .newsrc             .nn/rc  source           SRC/uucpbb          SRC/cnews, SRC/rn43 SRC/mnews  docs             DOC/uucpbb          DOC/cnews, DOC/rn   DOC/mnews, DOC/nn  UUCPbb   rnews, readnews, postnews, expire, subscribe, unsubscribe  C News   relaynews; bdecode, byteflip, c7decode, dbz, newshist,           newslock; newsrun, and trlf, written for this disk    rn     rn, Pnews, newsetup  MNews    inews, rnews, sbatch, encode, decode; nn, nnmaster, nnaux,           nnadmin, nncheck, cvt_help; and `setup' in CMDScio ships in CMDS with Microware's permission, so all three run as theyare; DOC/README-CIO has the details.WHY ONE AND NOT TWO-------------------Two programs are called `rnews', and both carry that module name becausea remote site asks for it: uuxqt runs `rnews' by name, and whicheveranswers gets the news.  UUCP/rnews files each article under SPOOL/news;MNEWS/rnews hands each to MNews's inews.  With MNews's rnews resident,UUCPbb's postnews hands it a UUCPbb command line and it files nothing:  posting your article...locally...Syntax: rnews [<opts>] [<file>] [<opts>]Neither rnews is in CMDS itself, and a bare-name fork looks in theexecution directory, not along PATH.  `load' the one you chose, or copyit into your execution directory.  C News's own rnews is not installed;a batch reaches relaynews through SPOOL/CNEWS/in.coming and newsrun.The spools differ, and no reader sees another system's articles.  MNewswas written for /h0/SPOOL/NEWS, which RBF, ignoring case, treats asUUCPbb's SPOOL/news; so MNews here uses SPOOL/MNEWS, and C News usesSPOOL/CNEWS (SRC/mnews/README.OSK, SRC/cnews/README.OSK).readnews and rn keep your place in the same file, .newsrc in HOME, inthe same `group: numbers' form, but each numbers articles by its ownspool.  Run both from one home directory and each misreads the other's.nn uses .nn/rc.UUCPbb and MNews both reach other sites through UUCP: MNews's sbatchhands batches to `uux', and its rnews mails through CMDS/UUCP/rmail.UUCPbb's news -- beside UUCPbb's mail--------------------------------------  Part of UUCPbb 2.1, GPL v2; rnews and expire are from its patch 1.  Formatted manuals in DOC/uucpbb: readnews.man, postnews.man, rnews.man,  subscribe.man.    rnews        takes a batch apart and files each article; hands a                 compressed batch to `compress'    readnews     reads a prompt at a time: `y', `n', `q', and `h' for the                 commands.  Offers new groups one by one    postnews     asks for the header fields, posts locally and queues the                 article for your UUCP neighbour.  The Distribution must                 be listed in SYS/UUCP/distributions    expire       deletes articles past their date    subscribe, unsubscribe                 turn a group on or off in .newsrc  Needs: TERM set ("Unknown terminal type" without it); SPOOL/news in  place before rnews or readnews starts; UUCP/rnews resident for  postnews, which forks it by name -- otherwise      docmd_na(): cannot spawn process--> rnews -x0 -ncomp.os.os9 ...  and with it the posting lands as SPOOL/news/COMP/OS/OS9/a1.  Costs: no full-screen reader; all six need cio.  `unsubscribe' on a  group already off prints a number where the group's name should be.  To leave readnews: q.C News and rn -- the Unix pair------------------------------  C News, University of Toronto, 1985-1990: a news system without a  reader.  The OS-9 port came in GNU_ATP_1_40.lzh.  Use and  redistribution by anyone, with four conditions about credit  (DOC/cnews/COPYRIGHT).  rn 4.3: copy it in whole or in part, but do not  try to make money off it or pretend that you wrote it.    relaynews    files an article or batch under its newsgroup, numbers                 it, updates active and history, and refuses a                 Message-ID it has seen    newsrun      feeds relaynews everything in in.coming -- a post or a                 batch, compressed or not, Unix or OS-9 line endings    rn           the reader: `rn <group>', y reads, space pages, q leaves    Pnews        posts: asks for distribution, newsgroup and subject, or                 `Pnews -h <file> -s' sends a file with its header    newsetup     writes a .newsrc from the active file, every group                 unsubscribed    bdecode, c7decode   undo the encodings a batch uses to cross a link                        that loses the eighth bit    dbz, newshist       build the history index; look a message-id up    byteflip, newslock  reorder a dbz index made on another machine;                        take the news lock by hand  Configuration is USR/LIB/CNEWS (active, sys, history and its dbz  index, artnum); rn's library is USR/LIB/RN.  An article going round:      Pnews -h post -s    leaves it as SPOOL/CNEWS/in.coming/0local1      newsrun             files it as SPOOL/CNEWS/junk/1, removes it                          from in.coming      rn junk             offers `1 unread article in junk'  newsrun runs trlf and `compress' through your own OS-9's `shell', so  `load /dd/CMDS/NEWS/trlf' first.  For one file by hand,  `relaynews -r -n < SPOOL/CNEWS/in.coming/0local1' files it and leaves  it in in.coming.  rn needs:  1. A RAM disk at /r0 for scratch files; under os9exec,     `mount -r=256k /r0'.  Without it rn stops with `Can't open     /r0/rnvary.3', says `.newsrc restored', and leaves it renamed     .oldnewsrc.  Rename it back before trying again.  2. A .newsrc.  With none, rn runs newsetup through your own `shell'     (SYS/login loads it from /h1 under os9exec); with no shell it says     `Can't create a .newsrc--you must do it yourself'.  Running     `newsetup' yourself needs nothing else.  3. A News directory in HOME to save articles in; without it rn says     `Cannot make directory .../News' and saves them in HOME.  Costs: rn's `h' help pages were Unix shell scripts and are not here;  DOC/rn's manual page is the reference.  The six older tools need cio.  To leave rn: q.MNews and nn -- a news system and a full-screen reader------------------------------------------------------  MNews was written for OS-9 (1990-92), with a port of nn 6.3.  All  eleven are built here from SRC/mnews, trap-free.  Its terms ask that  the package travel whole, so SRC/mnews is complete; SOURCES.txt, under  `MNEWS AND NN', has them in full.    inews        files an article: `inews -h < article' posts one,                 `inews -c=newgroup:<group>' makes a group    rnews        unpacks a batch from another site, handing each article                 to inews    sbatch       batches articles for a neighbour and hands them to uux    nn           the reader: a full-screen menu by subject, threads, kill                 files, `?' for help    nnmaster     keeps nn's database, SPOOL/NN    nnaux        what nn runs to post, follow up or reply    nncheck      says whether there is unread news    nnadmin      looks inside nn's database; `nnadmin C' shows where                 everything is    encode, decode, cvt_help                 B News 2.11's seven-bit coder; nn's help converter  Needs, in order:  1. `setup', once each time the system starts.  Every MNews program     reads the SysInfo table -- host name, spool directories, locks --     which setup builds from SYS/sysinfo.  Without it: `inews: Can't     initialize the mess'.  2. Your own OS-9's `shell', for nnmaster (SYS/login loads it from /h1     under os9exec).  Without it `nnmaster -I' prints `Initializing     master data base...' and never returns.  With it, `nnmaster -I'     (answer OK, then y to reuse the GROUPS file) and then `nnmaster'     build nn's database.  nnmaster runs as its module's owner and makes     GROUPS readable by that owner only; anyone else's nn and nncheck     then stop with `Cannot open file .../GROUPS'.  As the super-user,     follow `nnmaster -I' with         attr -pr /h0/SPOOL/NN/GROUPS  3. inews resident before rnews runs: rnews forks `inews' by bare name,     and without it repeats `Can't execute 'inews'' until stopped.     `load /dd/CMDS/MNEWS/inews' and the batch reports `Received 1     articles'.  4. uux resident before sbatch, for the same reason; otherwise `Can't     fork 'uux ...' (errno = 221)'.  5. For a compressed batch, a module named `uncompress'; rnews forks it     by that name.  6. HOME and USER set, and TERM for nn.  nn's first run makes .nn in     HOME by running `makdir' through `shell' -- your own OS-9's, which     SYS/login loads from /h1 under os9exec.  Without them nn still opens     its welcome screen but makes no .nn, and nncheck keeps saying `nn     has not been invoked to initialize user files'.  Costs: the largest of the three, and the most steps before the first  article.  inews writes the Date: year as years since 1900: `Date: 23  Sep 126' for 2026.  To leave nn: Q.CHOOSING--------  You want threads, kill files and online help           MNews + nn  You have no cio and cannot get one                     MNews + nn, or                                                         relaynews and rn  You want rn                                            C News + rn  You run UUCPbb for mail and want news beside it        UUCPbb  You want the fewest steps before the first article     UUCPbb  Taking MNews with nn means the whole of CMDS/MNEWS, with `setup',  SYS/sysinfo, USR/LIB/NEWS, USR/LIB/NN and the SPOOL directories.  Run  `setup' first, and load inews before rnews.